Terraform provides three primary lifecycle meta-arguments that allow you to control how a resource is created, updated, or destroyed.
For completeness, the three lifecycle rules are:

1. create_before_destroy
2. prevent_destroy
3. ignore_changes

TL;DR — Terraform lifecycle meta-arguments
They control how Terraform creates, updates, or destroys a resource.

1. create_before_destroy → Create the new resource first, then delete the old one (zero/minimal downtime strategy).
2. prevent_destroy → Blocks accidental deletion (safety lock).
3. ignore_changes → Tells Terraform to ignore certain attribute changes (don’t trigger updates for them).

In one sentence: They modify Terraform’s default replacement and update behavior to give you safer and more controlled infrastructure changes. Or in simple word they control the behavior of the resrouces getting provisoned. 

SCENERIO:

+======================================================================+
|           TERRAFORM LIFECYCLE: create_before_destroy                 |
+======================================================================+

WHAT IT DOES
------------
create_before_destroy = true

-> Creates the NEW resource first
-> Then destroys the OLD resource
-> Prevents downtime during replacement


DEFAULT BEHAVIOR (WITHOUT LIFECYCLE)
------------------------------------

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "app_server" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.medium"
}

When instance_type changes:
1) Destroy old instance
2) Create new instance

Result: DOWNTIME


WITH create_before_destroy
--------------------------

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "app_server" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.large"

  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
  }
}

What happens:
1) Terraform creates the NEW instance first
2) Waits until it is fully provisioned
3) Then destroys the OLD instance

Result: ZERO or MINIMAL downtime


REAL-WORLD ANALOGY
------------------
BAD:
- Cancel apartment lease
- Then look for new apartment

GOOD:
- Rent new apartment
- Move furniture
- Then cancel old lease


WHEN TO USE
-----------
- Production EC2 instances
- Databases
- Load-balanced services
- Customer-facing applications


DEVOPS CONCEPT
--------------
Enables blue-green style replacement
instead of destructive replacement.
+======================================================================+

+======================================================================+
|               TERRAFORM LIFECYCLE: prevent_destroy                   |
+======================================================================+

WHAT IT DOES
------------
prevent_destroy = true

-> Prevents Terraform from destroying a resource
-> Throws an error if a destroy is attempted
-> Acts as a safety lock


DEFAULT BEHAVIOR (WITHOUT LIFECYCLE)
------------------------------------

Example:

resource "aws_db_instance" "prod_db" {
  allocated_storage = 20
  engine            = "mysql"
}

If someone runs:
terraform destroy

Result:
Database gets deleted.


WITH prevent_destroy
--------------------

Example:

resource "aws_db_instance" "prod_db" {
  allocated_storage = 20
  engine            = "mysql"

  lifecycle {
    prevent_destroy = true
  }
}

If someone runs:
terraform destroy

Result:
Terraform throws an ERROR.
Resource cannot be destroyed.


REAL-WORLD ANALOGY
------------------
- Like putting a safety lock on a nuclear launch button.
- You must intentionally remove the lock before action.


WHEN TO USE
-----------
- Production databases
- Critical storage buckets
- Stateful systems
- Anything that must NEVER be deleted accidentally


DEVOPS CONCEPT
--------------
Adds a protection guardrail
against accidental infrastructure loss.
+======================================================================+

+======================================================================+
|                TERRAFORM LIFECYCLE: ignore_changes                   |
+======================================================================+

WHAT IT DOES
------------
ignore_changes = [attribute]

-> Tells Terraform to ignore changes to specific attributes
-> Prevents unnecessary updates or replacements
-> Useful when values are modified outside Terraform


DEFAULT BEHAVIOR (WITHOUT LIFECYCLE)
------------------------------------

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "app_server" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.medium"
  tags = {
    Name = "AppServer"
  }
}

If someone manually changes a tag in AWS console:

Result:
Terraform detects drift and tries to fix it.


WITH ignore_changes
-------------------

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "app_server" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.medium"

  tags = {
    Name = "AppServer"
  }

  lifecycle {
    ignore_changes = [tags]
  }
}

If tags change outside Terraform:

Result:
Terraform ignores the change.
No update triggered.


REAL-WORLD ANALOGY
------------------
- Like telling Terraform:
  "Don’t worry about this part. Leave it alone."


WHEN TO USE
-----------
- Auto-managed tags
- Attributes changed by external systems
- Cloud provider auto-updated fields


DEVOPS CONCEPT
--------------
Controls drift detection behavior
by selectively ignoring attribute changes.
